\begin{proposition}[Topological No-Schism Principle]\label{ch01:prop:topological-no-schism}%
\uses{ch01:def:adm-pareto-frontier}%
Let $\mathcal{M} \subseteq \wp(\mathbb{D})$ be a Moore extent family and let $\mathcal{F} := \{R \in \mathcal{M} \mid K \subseteq R \subseteq T\}$ be its Living-Zone poset relative to {{ref:ch01:def:adm-pareto-frontier}}. If $\mathcal{M}$ is closed under binary set-theoretic unions ($E_1, E_2 \in \mathcal{M} \implies E_1 \cup E_2 \in \mathcal{M}$), then $\mathcal{F}$ has at most one inclusion-maximal element:
\[
  R_1, R_2 \in \mathrm{Max}(\mathcal{F}) \implies \forall d \in \mathbb{D},\; (d \in R_1 \iff d \in R_2).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Constructively without axioms: if $R_1, R_2 \in \mathrm{Max}(\mathcal{F})$, binary union closure places $R_1 \cup R_2 \in \mathcal{M}$, and $K \subseteq R_1 \subseteq R_1 \cup R_2 \subseteq T$ gives $R_1 \cup R_2 \in \mathcal{F}$. Maximality of $R_1$ and $R_2$ forces $R_1 \cup R_2 \subseteq R_1$ and $R_1 \cup R_2 \subseteq R_2$, yielding $R_1 = R_2$. By contrast, {{sym:j-ext}}-multiplicativity requires extents to be closed under doctrinal products $d_1 \times d_2$ rather than arbitrary binary unions: when $d_1 \in E_1 \setminus E_2$ and $d_2 \in E_2 \setminus E_1$, $d_1 \times d_2$ need not lie in $E_1 \cup E_2$, which breaks union closure and unlocks multi-dimensional Pareto Schisms ($|{{sym:pareto-res}}| \ge 2$).
\end{proof}

